 ## Overview
-GPU-driven visibility culling is a technique that uses per-subchunk metadata (AABB, terrain/water draw ranges) to test visibility on the GPU and emit indirect draw commands only for visible subchunks [S1, unofficial]. It differs from CPU-side culling by offloading the visibility test and command buffer population to the GPU, allowing the CPU to remain free for scene management and chunk loading [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Task Shader Block-wise Stream Compaction](1540-task-shader-block-wise-stream-compaction.md) by utilizing the metadata prepared there to drive the compute dispatch and indirect execution.
+GPU-driven visibility culling determines a potentially visible set (PVS) of subchunks to avoid extracting geometry for blocks not visible in the display, reducing the number of task shader workgroups launched. It differs from CPU-side culling by offloading the visibility test and command buffer population to the GPU, allowing the CPU to remain free for scene management and chunk loading. It builds on [Task Shader Block-wise Stream Compaction](1540-task-shader-block-wise-stream-compaction.md) by utilizing the metadata prepared there to drive the compute dispatch and indirect execution.
 
